WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.981
We now have fast operations at

2
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.570
the front of a linked list

3
00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.510
and adding at the end is fast too

4
00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.260
thanks to the tail reference.

5
00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.600
So removing the last item should be easy

6
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:14.160
right?

7
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.680
We even know where it is

8
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.890
tail points straight at it.

9
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.360
Let's try.

10
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.652
It turns out not to be easy at all

11
00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:24.854
and fixing that gives us two

12
00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.020
new kinds of linked list.

13
00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.462
In this video we'll see why removing

14
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.020
the last node is slow

15
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.900
the doubly linked list

16
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.890
and the circularly linked list.

17
00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:39.160
To remove D, the node before it, C

18
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.140
must stop pointing to D.

19
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.180
So C's next has to become empty

20
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.270
and tail has to move to C.

21
00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.390
But we don't have C.

22
00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:52.610
D has no arrow back to its predecessor

23
00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.140
and tail only points at D.

24
00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.980
The only way to reach C is to start at head

25
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.402
and walk, one arrow at a time

26
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.896
until we're standing on the node

27
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.170
whose next is the tail.

28
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.610
For a list of n items

29
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.485
that's n minus two hops, so about n.

30
00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.167
Removing the last node of a singly linked

31
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.040
list takes time proportional to n.

32
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.510
The fix is to remember the predecessor.

33
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.430
Give every node a second reference

34
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.740
pointing backwards.

35
00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.577
Now a node knows both its next

36
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.360
and its previous node.

37
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.650
This is a doubly linked list.

38
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.410
Removing a node we hold is easy.

39
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.594
Its predecessor's next must skip over it

40
00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.524
and its successor's previous

41
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.200
must skip over it.

42
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.074
Two references changed.

43
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.503
It doesn't matter if the

44
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.100
node is in the middle, or at the end

45
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.790
the cost is the same, constant time.

46
00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.117
One thing is awkward.

47
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.751
The first node has no predecessor and

48
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.506
the last node has no successor

49
00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.349
so the code needs special

50
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.810
cases for both ends, and for an empty list.

51
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.070
A neat trick removes them.

52
00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:07.050
We add two dummy nodes that never hold data

53
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.210
a header at the front

54
00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.160
and a trailer at the back.

55
00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.660
Now every real node

56
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.340
even the first and the last

57
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.282
has a node on each side.

58
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.693
The list is empty when the header's

59
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.750
next is the trailer.

60
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.012
The real items always sit between

61
00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.340
the two sentinels.

62
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.051
Inserting and deleting never

63
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.420
need a special case.

64
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.510
Inserting is also one operation

65
00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:31.742
for any position

66
00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.800
insert a new node between two neighbours

67
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:35.160
that are already linked.

68
00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:39.640
A new node X, between B and C.

69
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:45.120
First, X's previous is B, and X's next is C

70
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.630
the new node points out to its neighbours.

71
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.650
Only then do the neighbours change.

72
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.570
B's next becomes X.

73
00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.140
And C's previous becomes X.

74
00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.020
Four references.

75
00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.600
The order has the same logic as before

76
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.400
connect the new node first

77
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.056
then change the old ones.

78
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.406
While the old ones still

79
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.010
point at each other, the list stays intact.

80
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.789
Adding at the front is inserting between

81
00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.037
the header and the first node.

82
00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.312
Adding at the end is inserting between

83
00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.470
the last node and the trailer.

84
00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:18.760
Same operation.

85
00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.503
Doubly linked lists aren't free.

86
00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.220
Every node carries two references

87
00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:24.270
instead of one

88
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.220
so the list takes more memory.

89
00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.232
And every insertion or deletion updates

90
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.320
twice as many references.

91
00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.118
Forget one, and the list can end up pointing

92
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.290
in two inconsistent ways.

93
00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.490
So we use the second link when we need it

94
00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:41.590
when we must remove from the end

95
00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.840
or remove a node we already hold.

96
00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.207
A different change, for a different problem.

97
00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.029
What if the last node doesn't

98
00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:50.290
point to nothing

99
00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.220
but back to the first node?

100
00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.600
Now the list is a ring.

101
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:55.715
There is no end

102
00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.556
and following next from any node eventually

103
00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.570
brings us back to where we started.

104
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.150
We only need one reference now, tail.

105
00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.670
The head is simply tail's next.

106
00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:09.320
So both ends are one step away.

107
00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.020
Adding at the front

108
00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.240
the new node points to the head

109
00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:16.620
and tail's next points to the new node.

110
00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.360
Adding at the end is the same move

111
00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.470
and then tail steps forward to the new node.

112
00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.950
And we get a free operation: rotate.

113
00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.070
Move tail one step forward

114
00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.550
and the old head becomes the last item.

115
00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:31.620
Constant time.

116
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.610
Think of three people sharing a resource

117
00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.840
taking turns, round and round.

118
00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.280
Serve the person at the front.

119
00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.487
Then rotate

120
00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.096
and the person who was just served

121
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.030
goes to the back of the line.

122
00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:49.570
Ann, then Bo, then Cy, then Ann again.

123
00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.210
The ring never runs out

124
00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.830
it just keeps turning.

125
00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.780
This is called round-robin.

126
00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:14.952
Let's compare.

127
00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:17.824
Remember that all three can add and remove

128
00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.180
at the front in constant time.

129
00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:21.960
Adding at the end

130
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.960
constant time in all three

131
00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.730
when we keep a tail reference.

132
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.010
Removing at the end: singly linked

133
00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.330
proportional to n.

134
00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.680
Doubly linked, constant time.

135
00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:37.740
Removing a node we already hold: singly, n

136
00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.400
because we must find its predecessor.

137
00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:42.070
Doubly, constant.

138
00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.530
The circular list keeps just one reference

139
00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.550
and rotating takes a single assignment.

140
00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:51.940
It still can't remove the last node quickly.

141
00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:55.203
And memory: one reference per node for

142
00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:58.770
singly and circular, two for doubly.

143
00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.359
To sum up.

144
00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.688
A singly linked list can't find

145
00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.200
a node's predecessor

146
00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.710
so removing the last node costs n.

147
00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.114
A doubly linked list stores

148
00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.650
the predecessor too

149
00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:14.820
and two sentinels remove the special cases.

150
00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:18.340
A circular list keeps one tail reference

151
00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.930
and rotating is a single assignment.

152
00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.400
Now let's build both in code.

153
00:06:24.027 --> 00:06:26.508
Let's build a doubly linked list and

154
00:06:26.577 --> 00:06:29.507
a circularly linked list in Python.

155
00:06:30.087 --> 00:06:33.237
The node now has three fields: the value

156
00:06:33.237 --> 00:06:34.857
prev and next.

157
00:06:34.857 --> 00:06:36.967
Both references default to None.

158
00:06:37.547 --> 00:06:40.237
The list creates two sentinel nodes

159
00:06:40.237 --> 00:06:42.217
with None as their value.

160
00:06:42.217 --> 00:06:43.857
Header's next is the trailer

161
00:06:43.857 --> 00:06:46.147
and trailer's previous is the header.

162
00:06:46.497 --> 00:06:49.247
An empty list is exactly that

163
00:06:49.247 --> 00:06:51.827
header and trailer, linked to each other

164
00:06:51.827 --> 00:06:53.097
and size zero.

165
00:06:53.677 --> 00:06:56.448
Everything is built on two methods.

166
00:06:56.527 --> 00:06:58.849
Insert between creates the node with its

167
00:06:58.907 --> 00:07:01.627
prev and next set in the constructor.

168
00:07:01.627 --> 00:07:04.327
Then pred's next, and succ's prev

169
00:07:04.327 --> 00:07:05.307
are pointed at it.

170
00:07:05.307 --> 00:07:08.077
That is the four references, in order.

171
00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:10.317
It returns the new node

172
00:07:10.317 --> 00:07:12.017
so callers can keep it

173
00:07:12.017 --> 00:07:14.227
and remove it later in constant time.

174
00:07:14.577 --> 00:07:16.927
Delete node is the two-line mirror

175
00:07:16.927 --> 00:07:19.547
the previous node's next skips over it

176
00:07:19.547 --> 00:07:22.167
and the next node's prev skips over it.

177
00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:23.937
We return the value.

178
00:07:24.517 --> 00:07:28.349
The four end operations are one-liners.

179
00:07:28.447 --> 00:07:30.062
Add first inserts between

180
00:07:30.127 --> 00:07:31.797
the header and its next.

181
00:07:31.867 --> 00:07:34.556
Add last inserts between the trailer's

182
00:07:34.627 --> 00:07:36.877
prev and the trailer.

183
00:07:37.227 --> 00:07:40.397
Remove first deletes the header's next

184
00:07:40.397 --> 00:07:42.977
remove last deletes the trailer's prev.

185
00:07:42.977 --> 00:07:45.467
Both check for an empty list first.

186
00:07:45.817 --> 00:07:48.592
Notice there are no special cases for

187
00:07:48.667 --> 00:07:51.087
an empty list or a one-item list.

188
00:07:51.087 --> 00:07:53.417
That is what the sentinels bought us.

189
00:07:53.997 --> 00:07:56.147
We add B, C

190
00:07:56.147 --> 00:07:58.967
D at the end and A at the front.

191
00:07:58.967 --> 00:08:02.587
We hold on to the node for C, delete it

192
00:08:02.587 --> 00:08:05.237
and C comes back as the return value.

193
00:08:05.587 --> 00:08:08.637
The list is now A, B, D.

194
00:08:08.637 --> 00:08:13.317
Then remove last returns D, leaving A and B.

195
00:08:13.317 --> 00:08:15.867
We never walked the list to do any of that.

196
00:08:16.447 --> 00:08:18.357
Now the circular list.

197
00:08:18.357 --> 00:08:21.257
It reuses the singly linked Node

198
00:08:21.257 --> 00:08:22.807
and keeps only a tail.

199
00:08:23.157 --> 00:08:25.447
Add first has two cases.

200
00:08:25.447 --> 00:08:27.127
If the list is empty

201
00:08:27.127 --> 00:08:28.827
the node points to itself

202
00:08:28.827 --> 00:08:30.647
and it is the tail.

203
00:08:30.647 --> 00:08:33.247
Otherwise it is linked right after the tail

204
00:08:33.247 --> 00:08:34.757
which is the front of the ring.

205
00:08:35.107 --> 00:08:36.915
Add last is add first

206
00:08:36.997 --> 00:08:38.683
followed by moving the tail one

207
00:08:38.737 --> 00:08:40.867
step forward to the new node.

208
00:08:41.447 --> 00:08:44.077
The head is tail's next.

209
00:08:44.077 --> 00:08:46.577
Remove first returns its value

210
00:08:46.577 --> 00:08:48.837
and makes tail's next skip it.

211
00:08:48.837 --> 00:08:50.837
If the head is the tail

212
00:08:50.837 --> 00:08:52.577
it was the only node

213
00:08:52.577 --> 00:08:54.167
and the list becomes empty.

214
00:08:54.517 --> 00:08:56.747
And rotate is one assignment

215
00:08:56.747 --> 00:08:58.557
tail becomes tail's next.

216
00:09:03.817 --> 00:09:05.107
One trap.

217
00:09:05.107 --> 00:09:08.267
In a singly linked list, we stop at None.

218
00:09:08.267 --> 00:09:12.004
A ring has no None, so a loop that waits for

219
00:09:12.087 --> 00:09:14.297
the end never finishes.

220
00:09:14.647 --> 00:09:18.537
So the iterator walks exactly size nodes

221
00:09:18.537 --> 00:09:20.767
starting at the head, and stops.

222
00:09:21.347 --> 00:09:24.857
Let's serve Ann, Bo and Cy in turns.

223
00:09:24.857 --> 00:09:29.307
Four turns: Ann, Bo, Cy, and Ann again.

224
00:09:29.657 --> 00:09:32.847
Each turn reads the head, then rotates.

225
00:09:32.847 --> 00:09:34.537
Constant time every time.

226
00:09:35.117 --> 00:09:37.956
So in Python, the sentinels give a clean

227
00:09:38.027 --> 00:09:39.487
doubly linked list

228
00:09:39.487 --> 00:09:42.157
and a tail alone gives a ring.

229
00:09:42.737 --> 00:09:44.617
That completes chapter two.

230
00:09:44.687 --> 00:09:46.921
We stored a list in two completely

231
00:09:46.987 --> 00:09:48.057
different ways.

232
00:09:48.407 --> 00:09:51.317
An array: items side by side

233
00:09:51.317 --> 00:09:53.477
instant access by index

234
00:09:53.477 --> 00:09:55.477
expensive insertion in the middle.

235
00:09:55.477 --> 00:09:58.374
A dynamic array grows by doubling

236
00:09:58.457 --> 00:10:00.255
so that appending is constant

237
00:10:00.317 --> 00:10:01.647
time on average.

238
00:10:01.997 --> 00:10:05.875
A linked list: items joined by references

239
00:10:05.967 --> 00:10:07.142
cheap changes at the front

240
00:10:07.187 --> 00:10:09.247
and at any node we hold

241
00:10:09.247 --> 00:10:11.687
but a walk to reach the middle.

242
00:10:11.687 --> 00:10:13.887
Doubly linked: both ends.

243
00:10:13.887 --> 00:10:15.517
Circular: a ring.

244
00:10:15.867 --> 00:10:18.249
There is no best structure

245
00:10:18.337 --> 00:10:20.479
only the right one for the operations

246
00:10:20.537 --> 00:10:22.187
your program does most.

247
00:10:22.537 --> 00:10:25.903
Next chapter: stacks, queues and deques.

248
00:10:25.987 --> 00:10:27.619
They are defined by what

249
00:10:27.687 --> 00:10:30.927
you are allowed to do, and, you'll see

250
00:10:30.927 --> 00:10:33.377
we build them from exactly these two ideas.

